# top-crim-02-01: Punishments in BNS & Community Service (10 MCQs)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-031", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "How many classes of punishments are recognized under Section 4 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023?", ["Four", "Five", "Six", "Seven"], 2, "C", "Section 4 BNS recognizes six punishments: Death, Imprisonment for life, Imprisonment (rigorous/simple), Forfeiture of property, Fine, and Community Service.", {"A": "Four was under old English law.", "B": "Five was under Section 53 IPC.", "C": "Correct; BNS introduces Community Service as the 6th punishment.", "D": "Seven is incorrect."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 4", "Identify the six punishments in BNS 2023."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-032", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Which innovative punishment was introduced for the first time in Indian general penal law under Section 4(f) BNS 2023?", ["Caning / Whipping", "Community Service", "Transportation for life", "Banishment from district"], 1, "B", "Section 4(f) BNS introduces Community Service as an alternative reformative punishment for petty offences.", {"A": "Abolished in 1955.", "B": "Correct; Community Service is newly introduced.", "C": "Repealed in 1955.", "D": "Banishment is unconstitutional under Art 19."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 4(f)", "Recall the 6th punishment in BNS."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-033", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "In which of the following offences does the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 explicitly provide Community Service as an alternative punishment?", ["First-time theft where value of stolen property is less than Rs 5,000 upon return", "Robbery on highway between sunset and sunrise", "Kidnapping for ransom", "Voluntarily causing grievous hurt with weapon"], 0, "A", "Under Section 303(2) Proviso BNS, theft of value less than Rs 5,000 by a first-time offender where property is returned may be punished with Community Service.", {"A": "Correct; petty theft under Rs 5,000 has community service.", "B": "Heinous offence with 14 years rigorous imprisonment.", "C": "Punishable with death or life.", "D": "Severe bodily offence."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 303(2) Proviso", "Identify specific offences punishable with Community Service."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-034", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 356(2) BNS / Section 500 IPC, what new alternative punishment can the court award for Defamation?", ["Community Service", "Solitary confinement for 1 year", "Public apology in national newspaper", "Asset forfeiture"], 0, "A", "Section 356(2) BNS provides that whoever defames another may be punished with simple imprisonment up to 2 years, or with fine, or with both, or with community service.", {"A": "Correct; Community service is added for defamation.", "B": "Solitary confinement max 3 months.", "C": "Apology not statutory penalty in code.", "D": "Forfeiture not applicable to defamation."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 356(2)", "Understand community service in defamation."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-035", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 226 BNS (attempt to commit suicide to compel public servant), what punishment is prescribed?", ["Rigorous imprisonment up to 10 years", "Simple imprisonment up to one year, or fine, or both, or with community service", "No punishment as suicide attempt is decriminalized", "Capital punishment"], 1, "B", "Section 226 BNS penalizes attempting suicide with intent to compel or restrain any public servant from discharging official duty with simple imprisonment up to 1 year, fine, both, or community service.", {"A": "Severe punishment incorrect.", "B": "Correct; up to 1 year, fine, or community service.", "C": "General suicide attempt IPC 309 is omitted, but S. 226 penalizes coercive suicide attempts.", "D": "Capital punishment impossible."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 226", "Distinguish Section 226 BNS from legacy IPC Section 309."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-036", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Which of the following describes the meaning of 'Imprisonment for Life' in criminal law (Gopal Vinayak Godse / Maru Ram doctrine)?", ["Imprisonment for 14 years strictly", "Imprisonment for 20 years with remissions", "Imprisonment for the remainder of the natural life of the convict unless commuted by Government", "Imprisonment until age 65"], 2, "C", "A sentence of imprisonment for life means imprisonment for the remaining period of the convict's natural life, subject to statutory remission/commutation.", {"A": "Common misconception based on S. 57.", "B": "20 years is only for calculating fractions under S. 7 BNS / S. 57 IPC.", "C": "Correct; natural life of convict.", "D": "Retirement age irrelevant."}, "Medium", "Gopal Vinayak Godse, AIR 1961 SC 600", "Define the true legal duration of Life Imprisonment."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-037", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 7 BNS / Section 57 IPC, in calculating fractions of terms of punishment, imprisonment for life shall be reckoned as equivalent to imprisonment for:", ["14 years", "20 years", "25 years", "30 years"], 1, "B", "Section 7 BNS / Section 57 IPC explicitly provides that for calculating fractions of punishment, life imprisonment is reckoned as 20 years.", {"A": "14 years is minimum before remission under CrPC 433A / BNSS 475.", "B": "Correct; 20 years for fraction calculations.", "C": "25 years is incorrect.", "D": "30 years is incorrect."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 7 / IPC Section 57", "Recall calculation of fractions for life imprisonment."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-038", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 355 BNS / Section 510 IPC, misconduct in public by a drunken person is punishable with simple imprisonment up to 24 hours, or fine up to Rs 1,000, or with:", ["Rigorous imprisonment for 1 month", "Community service", "Solitary confinement", "Blacklisting of passport"], 1, "B", "Section 355 BNS adds community service as an alternative to 24-hour imprisonment or fine.", {"A": "Simple imprisonment only.", "B": "Correct; community service.", "C": "Solitary confinement not applicable.", "D": "Passport not code penalty."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 355", "Recognize community service in drunken misconduct."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-039", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 202 BNS / Section 168 IPC, a public servant unlawfully engaging in trade may be punished with simple imprisonment up to one year, or fine, or both, or with:", ["Community service", "Forfeiture of all pension benefits", "Life imprisonment", "Demotion by court decree"], 0, "A", "Section 202 BNS introduces community service as an alternative penalty for public servants unlawfully engaging in trade.", {"A": "Correct; community service is prescribed.", "B": "Pension is service rule, not code penalty.", "C": "Disproportionate.", "D": "Courts cannot order departmental demotion."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 202", "Identify community service in public servant offences."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-040", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-01", "Under Section 209 BNS / Section 174 IPC, non-attendance in obedience to an order from a public servant may be punished with simple imprisonment, fine, or with:", ["Solitary confinement", "Community service", "Public reprimand", "Attachment of passport"], 1, "B", "Section 209 BNS adds community service for non-attendance in obedience to legal summons or order.", {"A": "Not applicable.", "B": "Correct; community service.", "C": "Not in code.", "D": "Not in code."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 209", "Recall community service in procedural disobedience."))

# top-crim-02-02: Solitary Confinement & Commutation (10 MCQs)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-041", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "What is the absolute maximum period of Solitary Confinement that can be awarded in any single sentence under Section 11 BNS / Section 73 IPC?", ["One month", "Two months", "Three months", "Six months"], 2, "C", "Under Section 11 BNS / Section 73 IPC, solitary confinement shall in no case exceed three (3) months in the whole.", {"A": "Max for <= 6 month sentences.", "B": "Max for <= 1 year sentences.", "C": "Correct; absolute maximum is three months.", "D": "Exceeds statutory ceiling."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 11 / IPC Section 73", "Recall maximum limit of solitary confinement."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-042", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "If the term of rigorous imprisonment awarded is 8 months, what is the maximum solitary confinement the court can order?", ["One month", "Two months", "Three months", "Fourteen days"], 1, "B", "Scale under Section 11 BNS: term <= 6 months -> 1 month; term > 6 months and <= 1 year -> 2 months; term > 1 year -> 3 months. For 8 months, maximum is 2 months.", {"A": "For <= 6 months.", "B": "Correct; up to 2 months.", "C": "Only for > 1 year.", "D": "Execution limit, not award limit."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 11 Scale", "Apply solitary confinement scale to sentences."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-043", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "Under Section 12 BNS / Section 74 IPC, what is the maximum period of solitary confinement that can be executed at any one time?", ["7 days", "14 days", "21 days", "28 days"], 1, "B", "In executing solitary confinement, it shall in no case exceed fourteen (14) days at a time, with intervals of not less than such periods.", {"A": "For sentences > 3 months per month.", "B": "Correct; max 14 days at a time.", "C": "Exceeds limit.", "D": "Exceeds limit."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 12 / IPC Section 74", "Recall execution limits of solitary confinement."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-044", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "When the imprisonment awarded exceeds three months, what is the maximum solitary confinement that can be given in any ONE MONTH?", ["3 days", "7 days", "14 days", "21 days"], 1, "B", "Under Section 12 BNS / Section 74 IPC, when sentence exceeds 3 months, solitary confinement shall not exceed seven (7) days in any one month, with intervals between solitary periods.", {"A": "Too low.", "B": "Correct; maximum 7 days in any one month.", "C": "14 days is for <= 3 month sentences.", "D": "Too high."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 12 Proviso / IPC Section 74", "Apply monthly execution limits for long sentences."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-045", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "Can solitary confinement be awarded to a person sentenced to Simple Imprisonment?", ["Yes, if the judge records special reasons", "No, solitary confinement can ONLY be awarded where the sentence is Rigorous Imprisonment", "Yes, in offences against women", "Yes, if the convict consents in writing"], 1, "B", "Section 11 BNS / Section 73 IPC explicitly states that solitary confinement can be ordered only whenever any person is convicted of an offence for which the Court has power to sentence him to 'rigorous imprisonment'.", {"A": "No discretion.", "B": "Correct; strictly restricted to rigorous imprisonment.", "C": "Irrelevant.", "D": "Consent cannot confer illegal sentence."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 11 / IPC Section 73", "Master precondition of rigorous imprisonment for solitary confinement."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-046", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "Under Section 5 BNS / Section 54 IPC, who has the power to commute a sentence of death to any other sentence without the consent of the offender?", ["The High Court", "The Appropriate Government (Central or State Government)", "The Sessions Judge who passed the sentence", "The Chief Justice of India"], 1, "B", "The Appropriate Government may, without the consent of the offender, commute a sentence of death to any other punishment provided by the Sanhita.", {"A": "HC confirms or hears appeal, not commutation under S. 5.", "B": "Correct; Appropriate Government.", "C": "Judge is functus officio.", "D": "CJI has judicial powers, not executive commutation."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 5 / IPC Section 54", "Identify authority for commutation of death sentence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-047", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "In Sunil Batra v. Delhi Administration (1978), the Supreme Court restricted the imposition of solitary confinement, holding that:", ["Solitary confinement is completely unconstitutional under Article 21", "Prison authorities cannot impose solitary confinement bar without judicial court order, and it must comply with humane standards", "Solitary confinement can be awarded for 1 year in terrorist cases", "Convicts have no fundamental rights inside prison"], 1, "B", "The Supreme Court held that solitary confinement cannot be imposed by jail superintendents arbitrarily and must strictly conform to judicial orders under Sections 73/74.", {"A": "Not struck down completely; restricted.", "B": "Correct; judicial order mandatory, humane treatment.", "C": "Exceeds 3-month statutory cap.", "D": "Prisoners retain residual fundamental rights."}, "Hard", "(1978) 4 SCC 494", "Apply constitutional limits to solitary confinement."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-048", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "Under Section 6 BNS / Section 55 IPC, a sentence of imprisonment for life may be commuted by the Appropriate Government to imprisonment for a term not exceeding:", ["10 years", "14 years", "20 years", "7 years"], 1, "B", "Section 6 BNS / Section 55 IPC provides that the Appropriate Government may commute life imprisonment to imprisonment for a term not exceeding fourteen (14) years or fine.", {"A": "Incorrect.", "B": "Correct; maximum 14 years.", "C": "20 years is fraction calculation.", "D": "7 years is too short."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 6 / IPC Section 55", "Recall statutory commutation ceiling for life imprisonment."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-049", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "What is the minimum period of actual imprisonment a life convict must undergo before premature release can be granted under CrPC S. 433A / BNSS S. 475 in capital offences?", ["7 years", "10 years", "14 years", "20 years"], 2, "C", "Under Section 433A CrPC / Section 475 BNSS, where life imprisonment was imposed for an offence for which death is one of the punishments, the convict shall not be released unless they have served at least fourteen (14) years of actual imprisonment.", {"A": "Too low.", "B": "Too low.", "C": "Correct; mandatory minimum 14 years actual custody.", "D": "20 years is fraction calculation."}, "Medium", "BNSS Section 475 / CrPC Section 433A", "Understand statutory minimum actual custody before remission."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-050", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-02", "Under Section 8(2) BNS / Section 65 IPC, the term of imprisonment in default of payment of fine shall not exceed:", ["One-half of maximum term of imprisonment for the offence", "One-fourth of the maximum term of imprisonment fixed for the offence", "One-third of the maximum term", "Three months in all cases"], 1, "B", "The term of imprisonment in default of fine shall not exceed one-fourth of the maximum term of imprisonment fixed for the offence.", {"A": "One-half is for attempt.", "B": "Correct; one-fourth of maximum term.", "C": "One-third is undertrial bail threshold in S. 479.", "D": "Fixed limit applies only where offence is punishable with fine only."}, "Easy", "BNS Section 8(2) / IPC Section 65", "Calculate default imprisonment limits for non-payment of fine."))

# top-crim-02-03: Section 106 BNS Caveat & Rash/Negligent Acts (10 MCQs)
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-051", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "Which provision of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 was EXCLUDED from the commencement notification on 1 July 2024?", ["Section 106(1)", "Section 106(2)", "Section 111", "Section 69"], 1, "B", "Under MHA Gazette S.O. 850(E), sub-section (2) of Section 106 (hit-and-run by rash/negligent driving without reporting) was excluded from coming into force on 1 July 2024.", {"A": "Section 106(1) is active and in force.", "B": "Correct; Section 106(2) hit-and-run was stayed.", "C": "Section 111 is in force.", "D": "Section 69 is in force."}, "Hard", "MHA Gazette Notification S.O. 850(E)", "Master the Section 106(2) BNS stay caveat."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-052", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "What is the maximum term of imprisonment under Section 106(1) BNS (in force from 1 July 2024) for causing death by rash or negligent act (non-doctors)?", ["Up to 2 years", "Up to 5 years and fine", "Up to 7 years", "Up to 10 years"], 1, "B", "Section 106(1) BNS increases the general punishment for causing death by rash or negligent act to imprisonment extending up to five (5) years and fine (up from 2 years in IPC 304A).", {"A": "2 years was under old IPC Section 304A.", "B": "Correct; up to 5 years and fine.", "C": "7 years is incorrect.", "D": "10 years is in Section 106(2) which is not in force."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 106(1)", "Identify enhanced punishment under Section 106(1) BNS."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-053", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "Under Section 106(1) Proviso BNS, if death is caused by a registered medical practitioner while performing medical procedure, the punishment is capped at:", ["Imprisonment up to 1 year", "Imprisonment up to 2 years and fine", "Imprisonment up to 5 years", "Only disciplinary reprimand by Medical Council"], 1, "B", "Section 106(1) Proviso expressly provides that if such act is done by a registered medical practitioner while performing medical procedure, he shall be punished with imprisonment up to two (2) years, and fine.", {"A": "Too low.", "B": "Correct; capped at 2 years and fine for doctors.", "C": "5 years is for general public.", "D": "Criminal liability exists, but capped at 2 years."}, "Hard", "BNS Section 106(1) Proviso", "Master doctor medical negligence cap in BNS."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-054", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "What does Section 106(2) BNS (currently held in abeyance) prescribe for causing death by rash/negligent driving and escaping without reporting?", ["Imprisonment up to 5 years", "Imprisonment extending up to ten (10) years and fine", "Life imprisonment", "Community service only"], 1, "B", "Section 106(2) prescribes imprisonment up to 10 years and fine for escaping without reporting to police or magistrate soon after the incident.", {"A": "5 years is Section 106(1).", "B": "Correct; up to 10 years and fine.", "C": "Not life.", "D": "Severe crime, not community service."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 106(2)", "Understand stayed hit-and-run provision details."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-055", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "In Jacob Mathew v. State of Punjab (2005), the Supreme Court laid down that to convict a doctor for criminal medical negligence under Section 304A IPC / S. 106 BNS, the degree of negligence must be:", ["Slight carelessness or error of judgment", "Gross negligence showing reckless disregard for human life", "Civil negligence under the Consumer Protection Act", "Any deviation from standard textbook procedures"], 1, "B", "A Three-Judge Bench held that gross negligence (Bolam test) is required for criminal liability; simple lack of care or accident does not attract criminal prosecution.", {"A": "Civil negligence only.", "B": "Correct; gross negligence is mandatory for criminal conviction.", "C": "Civil tort threshold is lower.", "D": "Professional discretion protects against minor deviations."}, "Hard", "(2005) 6 SCC 1", "Apply Jacob Mathew criminal medical negligence doctrine."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-056", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "What is the fundamental difference between 'Rashness' and 'Negligence' in criminal law (Cherubin Gregory v. State of Bihar)?", ["Rashness is intentional, negligence is accidental", "Rashness involves running a known risk with reckless indifference; negligence involves breach of duty to take care through inadvertence", "Rashness applies to driving only, negligence applies to doctors", "Rashness is civil, negligence is criminal"], 1, "B", "In rashness, the actor foresees the risk but recklessly gambles with consequence; in negligence, the actor fails to exercise the care a prudent person would observe.", {"A": "Neither is premeditated intention.", "B": "Correct; conscious risk-taking vs inadvertent breach of duty.", "C": "Both apply across offences.", "D": "Both have criminal applications."}, "Medium", "AIR 1964 SC 205", "Distinguish rashness from negligence."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-057", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "A sets a live uninsulated electric wire across his garden to prevent trespassers without any warning notice. B touches the wire in the dark and dies. A is guilty of:", ["Section 106 BNS / Section 304A IPC (rash act causing death)", "Section 103 BNS (Murder)", "No offence as B was a trespasser", "Accident under Section 18 BNS"], 0, "A", "In Cherubin Gregory, setting a naked live wire was held to be a rash and hazardous act under Section 304A IPC / Section 106 BNS.", {"A": "Correct; rash and hazardous act causing death.", "B": "No specific intention to kill B.", "C": "Trespassers cannot be met with deadly booby traps.", "D": "No proper care and caution; not accident."}, "Hard", "Cherubin Gregory, AIR 1964 SC 205", "Apply rashness doctrine to hazardous acts."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-058", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "Can Section 106 BNS / Section 304A IPC apply where death is caused with direct intention or knowledge?", ["Yes, if the victim contributed to negligence", "No; Section 106 applies ONLY to cases where death is caused without intention or knowledge of causing death", "Yes, in motor vehicle accidents only", "Yes, at prosecutor's election"], 1, "B", "Section 106 BNS / Section 304A IPC is an exclusionary provision: it applies only where the act does NOT amount to culpable homicide (i.e. neither intention nor knowledge of causing death is present).", {"A": "Contributory negligence is no defence in crime.", "B": "Correct; requires absence of intention and knowledge of culpable homicide.", "C": "Applies across all non-intentional fatal acts.", "D": "Statutory elements govern, not election."}, "Medium", "BNS Section 106(1) text", "Understand exclusionary nature of Section 106 BNS."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-059", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "Under Section 106(1) BNS, what type of actus reus is required?", ["Direct, proximate and efficient cause of death without intervention of another contributory cause", "Remote cause of death", "Moral cause of death", "Supervening medical cause"], 0, "A", "In Emperor v. Omkar Rampratap, Sir Lawrence Jenkins held the act must be the direct, proximate, and immediate cause of death, causa causans, not merely causa sine qua non.", {"A": "Correct; direct proximate cause (causa causans).", "B": "Remote cause insufficient.", "C": "Moral cause not recognized.", "D": "Supervening cause breaks chain."}, "Hard", "(1902) 4 Bom LR 679", "Apply proximate cause test in Section 106."))
mcqs.append(q("mcq-crim-02-060", "ch-crim-02", "top-crim-02-03", "If an accident occurs on 15 August 2024 and the driver causes death by rash driving, under which provision will he be charged given the MHA Gazette S.O. 850(E)?", ["Section 106(1) BNS", "Section 106(2) BNS", "Section 304A IPC", "Section 302 IPC"], 0, "A", "Because Section 106(2) is stayed and not in force, and the offence occurred after 1 July 2024, the driver must be charged under Section 106(1) BNS (up to 5 years).", {"A": "Correct; Section 106(1) is the operational law.", "B": "Section 106(2) is stayed and not in force.", "C": "IPC was repealed for post-1 July 2024 acts.", "D": "No intention to murder shown."}, "Hard", "Statutory Intersection: BNS 106(1) vs 106(2)", "Determine applicable hit-and-run charge post-July 2024."))

# Save checkpoint
print(f"Chapter 2 complete: {len(mcqs)} MCQs.")
